course: dictionary
lesson: 735
title: Basis: what something is built on
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A basis is the main idea, fact or thing that something is built on or starts from. Think of it as the foundation under a house. «Humanists use the Golden Rule as a reasoned basis for treating others well, without needing belief in God.» The Golden Rule is where their good behaviour starts, so it is the basis for it.
explain: Use the words around basis to work it out. «Japan has strict earthquake rules, and many buildings have flexible frames or shock-absorbing bases» tells you the bases are the parts that stand on the ground and let a building move. Ask: what word would fit here? Try "foundation" or "starting point" in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Basis can also mean how often something is done. «On a daily basis» means every day, and «on a regular basis» means regularly. Here the word is about a pattern, not a thing underneath. The context tells you which meaning you need.
explain: Basis looks like base, and its plural is bases, so be careful. In «a run means getting round all four bases» in baseball, bases are the places a runner touches. The sentence tells you which meaning is meant, so always read the whole sentence.
fact: A basis is the main idea, fact or thing that something is built on or starts from.
fact: «On a daily basis» means every day, and «on a regular basis» means regularly.
fact: «No basis for the rumour» means there is no good reason or evidence for it.
fact: The basis of a scientific theory is the evidence that supports it.
fact: Basis and base both have the plural bases, so use the context to tell which one is meant.
exercise: «Humanists use the Golden Rule as a reasoned basis for treating others well.» In this sentence, basis means the | name of a holiday | *starting point or reason that good behaviour is built on | loudest noise in the room | part of a shoe that you tie
exercise: «In baseball a run means getting round all four bases.» Which meaning is closest to bases here? | Tall towers that hold up a bridge | The main reasons for a decision | Ways of doing something every day | *The places a runner touches on the field
exercise: Ella says the school bus runs «on a daily basis». What does that mean? | *Every day | Only on Sundays | Very slowly | Whenever the driver feels like it
test: «Earthquake-proof buildings use steel frames, flexible joints and even rubber or sliding bases to soak up shaking.» What job do the bases do in this sentence? | Switch the lights on and off | Make the building taller | *Soak up the shaking | Paint the walls a bright colour
test: Rakesh says, «There is no basis for the rumour.» What does he mean? | Rakesh is an expert on rumours | *There is no good reason or evidence for it | The rumour is true and everyone knows it | The rumour is very long and hard to follow
exam: In science, what is the basis of a theory? | The final answer to every test | A rule that can never be questioned | A type of microscope used in labs | *The evidence that supports it
